<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nazism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as National Socialism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nazism, or National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>, was the far-right ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s party, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1933 to 1945. It combined extreme nationalism, racism and antisemitism with authoritarian rule. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> link its rise to the humiliations and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> imposed after the empire's defeat in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazism.jpg" alt="Nazism" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Institut_f%C3%BCr_Sexualwissenschaft_-_Bibliothek_1933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Historians trace the roots of Nazism to European political culture before 1914. Almost all its essential elements, Martin Broszat points out, were present in the radical protest movements of pre-1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>: a virulent antisemitism, a blood-and-soil ideology, the notion of a master race and the idea of territorial settlement in the East. Its ultranationalism came from pan-Germanism and the Völkisch movement, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> paramilitary groups formed after Germany's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> were the source of its cult of violence. The idea of an Aryan race rested on 19th-century racial theories, among them those of Arthur de Gobineau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Nazism, formally National Socialism, is the ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>. The German Workers' Party, founded in 1919, was renamed the National Socialist German Workers' Party in 1920, partly to use both left-wing and right-wing terms. Most scholars classify the ideology as far-right. It opposed liberal democracy and rejected Marxist class conflict, and advocated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a>, the leader principle, set out in Hitler's Mein Kampf.</p><p>Its core was a belief in a racial hierarchy with an Aryan master race at the top. The Nazis treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as a mixed race responsible for the exploitation of the Aryans and classed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> as subhuman. They sought a homogeneous community of the people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksgemeinschaft">Volksgemeinschaft</a>, and aimed to unite all Germans and to gain land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> under the doctrine of Lebensraum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> became the largest party in the German parliament in 1932, without a majority. In January 1933 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> appointed Hitler Chancellor, with the support of conservative nationalists who believed they could control him. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> and the Enabling Act the Nazis built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, political opponents and others were marginalised, imprisoned or murdered, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> millions, among them two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s Jewish population, were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat Nazism came to be widely regarded as evil. Only a few fringe racist groups, usually called neo-Nazis, describe themselves as its followers, and the use of Nazi symbols is illegal in many European countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nazism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war guilt clause of Versailles, Article 231, and the reparations it justified fuelled the resentments from which <b>National Socialism</b> later drew strength, which makes the German Empire's collapse arguably the most consequential imperial dissolution of the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…100 million victims is the estimate the Victims of Communism Memorial Foundation most commonly uses, though some of its own authors distanced themselves from it, helped legitimise comparisons of <b>Nazism</b> and Stalinism, and museums and monuments to the victims stand in Eastern Europe, Canada and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Salazar denounced fascism and <b>Nazism</b> as a “pagan Caesarism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Fascism also centres on the demonisation of “Others”; in <b>Nazism</b> this meant racial purity and belief in a master race.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nazism</b>, which Alter and Brown count with Fascist Italy as integral nationalism, was devoted to an Aryan race; its goals included lebensraum in Eastern Europe, eugenics and the eradication of human beings the Nazis deemed inferior, and it refused to count Jews and Romani as Aryan.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>During the German occupation of 1940 to 1945 the population was evacuated and the <b>Nazis</b> built labour camps there.</blockquote><a href="/state/alderney">Alderney</a></li></ul></section>
